1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,751
In a production setting you might be able to create the most perfect dbt pipeline

2
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:11,698
but there is one thing you don't have control over

3
00:00:11,698 --> 00:00:13,667
and these are your sources

4
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:21,110
The sources are in production many times managed by other teams or third parties

5
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:26,556
So you don't really have control on like in what format they come in

6
00:00:26,560 --> 00:00:31,512
if the format changes or if the source is fresh or stale

7
00:00:31,520 --> 00:00:35,608
So for example your third party or the other team

8
00:00:35,608 --> 00:00:38,236
it just stops updating the source

9
00:00:38,240 --> 00:00:42,867
We will take a look later at how you can test sources of course

10
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:49,596
but here I want to show you a dbt feature that helps you check for source freshness

11
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:54,560
We are going to create a simple configuration property to the source

12
00:00:54,560 --> 00:00:57,475
and with the help of this we will be able to

13
00:00:57,475 --> 00:01:01,189
check if the data in the source is fresh enough

14
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:05,192
Now how do you know if the data in a source is fresh

15
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:07,552
What you can do is that you define a column

16
00:01:07,552 --> 00:01:09,512
we will call it a loaded ad field

17
00:01:09,520 --> 00:01:11,400
and dbt will take a look at this column

18
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:13,280
in our case this will be the date column

19
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:21,678
and simply check how old the most recent date record in that column is

20
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:25,424
It's always a good practice to have a date column

21
00:01:25,424 --> 00:01:28,880
either for fact data or also for dimension data

22
00:01:28,880 --> 00:01:32,718
to have an update to that or create that column

23
00:01:32,718 --> 00:01:35,669
so you can see when your data gets stale

24
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:41,040
If you remember in earlier videos we added an extra row to row reviews

25
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:43,926
but based on how you're pacing out this course

26
00:01:43,926 --> 00:01:47,034
that might be maybe just a few minutes in the past

27
00:01:47,040 --> 00:01:50,145
but maybe a few days or even weeks in the past right

28
00:01:50,145 --> 00:01:54,494
Now we're going to implement a source freshness check and find it out

29
00:01:54,494 --> 00:01:58,570
This requires a few lines of code so let me just paste this here for you

30
00:01:58,570 --> 00:02:02,560
So what you want to do is that for the reviews column

31
00:02:02,560 --> 00:02:06,108
you create a new config and under the config

32
00:02:06,108 --> 00:02:11,268
you specify the loaded ad field which in our case remember is date right

33
00:02:12,080 --> 00:02:17,706
Date as the name of the column and then for the freshness

34
00:02:17,706 --> 00:02:23,821
we can decide how strict we want to be with the source freshness

35
00:02:23,821 --> 00:02:26,085
So we can say that for example

36
00:02:26,085 --> 00:02:30,051
if the most recent record in our source table

37
00:02:30,051 --> 00:02:33,877
is older than one hour in this case

38
00:02:33,877 --> 00:02:37,278
then we want to have a warning

39
00:02:37,278 --> 00:02:40,581
but if it's older than 24 hours

40
00:02:40,581 --> 00:02:42,557
then we want to have an error

41
00:02:42,557 --> 00:02:45,274
Let's save this file and save it in action

42
00:02:45,280 --> 00:02:50,965
I can say dbt source freshness and by executing this

43
00:02:50,965 --> 00:02:56,800
let's see how old my most recent record is in the table

44
00:02:56,800 --> 00:03:00,640
Oh no I received an error so it's very old

45
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:03,200
When you take this to production

46
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:06,035
you should know that if there is an error

47
00:03:06,035 --> 00:03:09,343
then the exit code of this dbt source freshness command

48
00:03:09,360 --> 00:03:11,028
will be non zero right

49
00:03:11,028 --> 00:03:13,530
So if I say give me the exit code

50
00:03:13,530 --> 00:03:17,150
it's 1 which means that if this was integrated

51
00:03:17,150 --> 00:03:20,741
into a CI CD pipeline then the pipeline would fail

52
00:03:20,741 --> 00:03:24,480
Now this specific comment is Linux and Mac specific

53
00:03:24,480 --> 00:03:29,920
but I'm giving you the Windows comment too in course resources

54
00:03:29,920 --> 00:03:32,209
So let me just comment this out

55
00:03:32,209 --> 00:03:35,992
so I can demo a warning for you okay Let's execute this

56
00:03:35,992 --> 00:03:38,737
Obviously our source will be stale

57
00:03:38,737 --> 00:03:42,082
it will be older than one hour in the past

58
00:03:42,082 --> 00:03:44,820
So now that gives me a warning

59
00:03:44,820 --> 00:03:47,366
that you can pick up from the logs if you want

60
00:03:47,366 --> 00:03:50,149
but if I ask if this source freshness failed

61
00:03:50,149 --> 00:03:52,205
then you will see a zero exit code

62
00:03:52,205 --> 00:03:55,018
which means that from the for example

63
00:03:55,018 --> 00:03:57,830
a CI CD point of view that's okay

64
00:03:57,840 --> 00:04:00,582
you don't need to fail the whole pipeline

65
00:04:00,582 --> 00:04:03,323
All in all you want to ensure that anytime

66
00:04:03,323 --> 00:04:05,085
you take dbt to production

67
00:04:05,085 --> 00:04:09,353
you want to apply source freshness on third party sources
